# /etc/systemd/system/uat.slice: every UAT unit runs inside this slice (CORE-165).
#
# Darko, 2026-11-23 08:50 EST (CORE-160 Rulings): UAT runs on the Beelink (town-rig: 16 cores, 27 GB), capped to
# prod's size, so a load run's numbers mean something for prod. Prod is a t3.xlarge: 4 vCPU, 16 GB.
#
# AllowedCPUs, not CPUQuota: four whole cores, the last four. Inside the slice, nproc and Node's availableParallelism
# answer 4, as on prod, so worker pools size as they do there, rather than seeing 16 cores and being throttled to 4.
# AllowedCPUs CONFINES UAT to cores 12-15; it does not keep anything else off them. The other twelve cores are the
# meeps' by habit, not by reservation: a meep or a system job may still land on 12-15, and then a load run's numbers
# are not prod's (CORE-165, finding 9). CPUWeight makes UAT win those cores when they are contended (siblings default
# to 100). It is a weight, not a reservation: a true reservation would be an AllowedCPUs=0-11 on system.slice and
# user.slice, a change to the box's other tenants that is a person's to make (README § Open). 500 is a judgment: five
# times a default sibling's share, so UAT is not starved on its four cores and the meeps, who have twelve others, are
# hardly slowed. MemoryMax is prod's RAM. MemoryHigh starts reclaim just under it, as a 16 GB box would. Swap is off,
# as on prod (no swap there).
[Unit]
Description=UAT: Postmark's frozen release, capped to prod's size (4 cores, 16 GB; CORE-165)
Before=slices.target

[Slice]
AllowedCPUs=12-15
CPUWeight=500
MemoryAccounting=yes
MemoryHigh=15G
MemoryMax=16G
MemorySwapMax=0
IOAccounting=yes
IOWeight=50
